   "source": [
    "**Launch this interactive notebook in Binder ⇒** [![Binder](https://mybinder.org/badge_logo.svg)](https://mybinder.org/v2/gh/arundo/adtk/master?filepath=docs%2Fnotebooks%2Fdemo.ipynb)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Detector"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### ThresholdAD"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "`ThresholdAD` compares each time series value with given thresholds.\n",
    "\n",
    "In the following example, we detect time points when temperature is above 30C or below 15C."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "s = pd.read_csv('./data/temperature.csv', index_col=\"Time\", parse_dates=True, squeeze=True)\n",
    "from adtk.data import validate_series\n",
    "s = validate_series(s)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from adtk.detector import ThresholdAD\n",
    "threshold_ad = ThresholdAD(high=30, low=15)\n",
    "anomalies = threshold_ad.detect(s)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1152x288 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from adtk.visualization import plot\n",
    "plot(s, anomaly=anomalies, ts_linewidth=1, ts_markersize=3, anomaly_markersize=5, anomaly_color='red', anomaly_tag=\"marker\");"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### QuantileAD"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "`QuantileAD` compares each time series value with historical quantiles.\n",
    "\n",
    "In the following example, we detect time points when temperature is above 99% percentile or below 1% percentile."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from adtk.detector import QuantileAD\n",
    "quantile_ad = QuantileAD(high=0.99, low=0.01)\n",
    "anomalies = quantile_ad.fit_detect(s)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1152x288 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plot(s, anomaly=anomalies, ts_linewidth=1, ts_markersize=3, anomaly_markersize=5, anomaly_color='red', anomaly_tag=\"marker\");"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### InterQuartileRangeAD"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "`InterQuartileRangeAD` is another widely used detector based on simple historical statistics is based on interquartile range (IQR). When a value is out of the range defined by $[Q_1 - c \\times IQR,\\  Q_3 + c \\times IQR]$ where $IQR = Q_3 - Q_1$ is the difference between 25% and 75% quantiles. This detector is usually preferred to QuantileAD in the case where only a tiny portion or even none of training data is anomalous."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from adtk.detector import InterQuartileRangeAD\n",
    "iqr_ad = InterQuartileRangeAD(c=1.5)\n",
    "anomalies = iqr_ad.fit_detect(s)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1152x288 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plot(s, anomaly=anomalies, ts_linewidth=1, ts_markersize=3, anomaly_markersize=5, anomaly_color='red', anomaly_tag=\"marker\");"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### GeneralizedESDTestAD"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "`GeneralizedESDTestAD` detects anomaly based on generalized extreme Studentized deviate (ESD) test.\n",
    "\n",
    "Please note a key assumption of generalized ESD test is that normal values follow an approximately normal distribution. Please only use this detector when this assumption holds."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from adtk.detector import GeneralizedESDTestAD\n",
    "esd_ad = GeneralizedESDTestAD(alpha=0.3)\n",
    "anomalies = esd_ad.fit_detect(s)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1152x288 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plot(s, anomaly=anomalies, ts_linewidth=1, ts_markersize=3, anomaly_markersize=5, anomaly_color='red', anomaly_tag=\"marker\");"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### PersistAD"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "`PersistAD` compares each time series value with its previous values. Internally, it is implemented as a [pipenet](#Pipenet) with transformer [DoubleRollingAggregate](#DoubleRollingAggregate).\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "In the following example, we detect anomalous positive changes of price."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "s = pd.read_csv('./data/price_short.csv', index_col=\"Time\", parse_dates=True, squeeze=True)\n",
    "s = validate_series(s)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 11,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1152x288 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from adtk.detector import PersistAD\n",
    "persist_ad = PersistAD(c=3.0, side='positive')\n",
    "anomalies = persist_ad.fit_detect(s)\n",
    "plot(s, anomaly=anomalies, ts_linewidth=1, ts_markersize=3, anomaly_color='red');"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "By default, `PersistAD` only check one previous value, which is good at capturing additive anomaly in short-term scale, but not in long-term scale because it is too near-sighted. \n",
    "\n",
    "In the following example, it fails to capture meaningful drops of price in a longer time scale."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "s = pd.read_csv('./data/price_long.csv', index_col=\"Time\", parse_dates=True, squeeze=True)\n",
    "s = validate_series(s)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 13,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1152x288 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "persist_ad = PersistAD(c=1.5, side='negative')\n",
    "anomalies = persist_ad.fit_detect(s)\n",
    "plot(s, anomaly=anomalies, anomaly_color='red');"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We may change the parameter `window` to a number greater than 1, and the detector will compare a value to the median or mean of its preceding time window. This will capture anomalous changes in mid- to long-term scale, because it is less near-sighted. In the same example as above, it detects drops of price in the long-term scale successfully."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 14,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "persist_ad.window = 24\n",
    "anomalies = persist_ad.fit_detect(s)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 15,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1152x288 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plot(s, anomaly=anomalies, anomaly_color='red');"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### LevelShiftAD"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "`LevelShiftAD` detects shift of value level by tracking the difference between median values at two sliding time windows next to each other. It is not sensitive to instantaneous spikes and could be a good choice if noisy outliers happen frequently. Internally, it is implemented as a [pipenet](#Pipenet) with transformer [DoubleRollingAggregate](#DoubleRollingAggregate).\n",
    "\n",
    "In the following example, we detect shift point of CPU usage."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 16,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "s = pd.read_csv('./data/cpu.csv', index_col=\"Time\", parse_dates=True, squeeze=True)\n",
    "s = validate_series(s)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 17,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1152x288 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from adtk.detector import LevelShiftAD\n",
    "level_shift_ad = LevelShiftAD(c=6.0, side='both', window=5)\n",
    "anomalies = level_shift_ad.fit_detect(s)\n",
    "plot(s, anomaly=anomalies, anomaly_color='red');"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### VolatilityShiftAD"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "`VolatilityShiftAD` detects shift of volatility level by tracking the difference between standard deviations at two sliding time windows next to each other. Internally, it is implemented as a [pipenet](#Pipenet) with transformer [DoubleRollingAggregate](#DoubleRollingAggregate).\n",
    "\n",
    "In the following example, we detect positive shift of volatility of seismic amplitude which indicates start of earthquake."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 18,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "s = pd.read_csv('./data/seismic.csv', index_col=\"Time\", parse_dates=True, squeeze=True)\n",
    "s = validate_series(s)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 19,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1152x288 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from adtk.detector import VolatilityShiftAD\n",
    "volatility_shift_ad = VolatilityShiftAD(c=6.0, side='positive', window=30)\n",
    "anomalies = volatility_shift_ad.fit_detect(s)\n",
    "plot(s, anomaly=anomalies, anomaly_color='red');"
   ]
